## Order Creation Rules

```typescript
// schemas/order.schema.ts
export const OrderInput = z.object({
  external_id: z.string().min(1),
  shipping: z.enum(['STANDARD', 'EXPRESS', 'PRINTFUL_FAST']).default('STANDARD'),
  recipient: z.object({
    name: z.string().min(1),
    address1: z.string().min(1),
    address2: z.string().optional(),
    city: z.string().min(1),
    state_code: z.string().length(3),    // MX: 'JAL', 'CMX', 'NLE', etc.
    country_code: z.literal('MX'),       // hardcoded for this project
    zip: z.string().regex(/^\d{5}$/),    // MX postal codes are 5 digits
    phone: z.string().min(10),
    email: z.string().email(),
    tax_number: z.string().optional(),   // ← RFC del cliente final si pidió factura
  }),
  items: z.array(z.object({
    sync_variant_id: z.number().int().positive(),
    quantity: z.number().int().positive(),
    retail_price: z.string(),
  })).min(1),
  retail_costs: z.object({
    currency: z.literal('MXN'),
    subtotal: z.string(),
    shipping: z.string(),
    tax: z.string(),
    total: z.string(),
  }),
});
```

**Mexican state codes (ISO 3166-2:MX):** AGU, BCN, BCS, CAM, CHP, CHH, CMX, COA, COL, DUR, GUA, GRO, HID, JAL, MEX, MIC, MOR, NAY, NLE, OAX, PUE, QUE, ROO, SLP, SIN, SON, TAB, TAM, TLA, VER, YUC, ZAC.

**Rule:** when implementing the address form, validate `state_code` against this set.

